## Build requirements

Deliver a complete, polished, playable game, not a tech demo. The **hard
requirements** — real graphics, no backend or credentials, the palette and type,
a `README.md`, and the game states — are stated in full in `specs/overview.md`
(and the files it points to). That specification is authoritative; build to it
rather than to a summary here. Three things bear restating, because the harness
depends on them:

- **The fixed build/serve interface.** The project stays a **Node project** built
  with only Node.js and npm-installed dependencies. Extend the provided
  `package.json` at `{{workspace}}` and **commit a `package-lock.json`** (run
  `npm install` after you change dependencies so the lockfile matches). `npm ci`
  then `npm run build` must produce the static site — with no other manual step —
  into one of `dist/`, `build/`, or `out/` at the project root, with an
  `index.html` at the root of that directory. Serving that directory as-is must
  run the game, and it must work **at any base path** (it is played back from a
  per-run sub-path, so any URL the build constructs at runtime — including every
  produced asset — must be page-relative, never a leading `/`; for a bundler, set
  a relative base such as `base: './'`). `specs/overview.md` states this in full;
  follow it exactly.
- **You PRODUCE the snake art and audio.** There is **no** pre-made game art or
  sound in this project. Instead, the run image puts asset-generation tools on your
  `PATH` — **`draw`** and **`draw-sheet`** (sprites and animated sprite sheets) and
  **`sfx-synth`**, **`sfx-sample`**, and **`music`** (sound and music) — and you
  must **produce the snake's sprite set and the game's audio with these tools
  during this build**, then wire the committed files into the game. Run each tool's
  `--help` to learn its operations. **`specs/assets.md` is the authoritative
  contract**: it enumerates the snake sprites (an animated biting head, a
  straight-body sprite, corner sprites, and a tail) and the sounds (eat / combo-up
  / death cues and a music bed), names which tool produces each and where it lands
  under `assets/`, and states how each is wired in. The board, walls, pellet, and
  HUD stay drawn in code. Your build must be **self-contained** — it
  bundles the committed files and must **not** invoke these tools at build time
  (they are gone when the build is re-run to validate it). Load every produced file
  with page-relative URLs so it resolves from the served sub-path.
- **The reference screenshots.** The `reference/` folder under `{{workspace}}` holds
  screenshots that are visual targets for the title, gameplay, and game-over
  screens — match their layout, palette, and type, but build the screens from the
  specification rather than copying the images.

## Verify before you finish

Your project has **Playwright** and a headless **Chromium** installed as project
dependencies, plus network access — use them to verify your build, do not just
assume it works. Before you consider the task complete:

- Produce the **static production build** and serve it locally, then open it in
  Chromium with Playwright and confirm it **loads with no console errors** and no
  uncaught exceptions.
- **Drive the game** — press the keys, start a round, eat pellets (watch the head
  bite and hear the cues), build and lose the combo, then run into a wall to reach
  the game-over screen — and **screenshot** the title, gameplay, and game-over
  screens. Confirm the snake renders from your **produced sprites** (a biting head,
  cornered turns, a tail) and that your **produced audio** plays. Compare each
  screen against the matching image in `reference/` and against the specs, and fix
  what does not match before finishing.
- **Capture the proof of implementation** exactly as `specs/proof.md` specifies:
  the screenshots and the short clip, each written to the precise `proof/` path
  that spec names. These are required deliverables of the finished build, not a
  private check — the same driven screens you screenshot above are what they
  record, plus the clip. Do not invent a different folder or filename, and do not
  skip the `.webm` clip.

Use the project's own Playwright: import it from your project (for example
`import { chromium } from "playwright"`) or run the project-local CLI with
`npx playwright`. There is **no** global Playwright on `PATH` — it lives in your
project's `node_modules`, like any other dependency. Launch Chromium with the
`--no-sandbox` argument — this container has no Chromium sandbox, so a default
launch will fail.

## Commit your work

Your project root is a **git repository** — it was initialized before you
started, with the starter files in its first commit. Your finished
implementation is published directly from this repository's commits, so work you
leave uncommitted is **not** published. When the build is complete and verified,
commit everything: run `git add -A` then `git commit`, and confirm `git status`
reports a clean working tree with nothing uncommitted or untracked. A
`.gitignore` already excludes `node_modules/` and the build output
(`dist/`/`build/`/`out/`), so a clean tree does not mean committing those — but
it does mean committing your source, your `package-lock.json`, your `README.md`,
the **produced assets under `assets/`**, and the `proof/` artifacts.
